{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":21867395,"ns":0,"title":"Ax–Grothendieck theorem","extract":"In mathematics, the Ax–Grothendieck theorem is a result about injectivity and surjectivity of polynomials that was proved independently by James Ax and Alexander Grothendieck.\nThe theorem is often given as this special case: If \n  \n    \n      \n        P\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle P}\n  \n is an injective polynomial function from an \n  \n    \n      \n        n\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle n}\n  \n-dimensional complex vector space to itself then \n  \n    \n      \n        P\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle P}\n  \n is bijective.","description":"Injective polynomial functions are bijective","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}